## Report
Validated the finding as a logic/authorization bug. Direct PoC execution did not crash, which is expected for this class of issue; valgrind was attempted but is not installed; lldb was then used successfully to trace the vulnerable path. Code evidence: bucket policies with Version 2012-10-17 enable variables at crates/auth/src/bucket_policy.rs:128. The affected negative string operator families are marked as supporting variables at crates/auth/src/bucket_policy/condition_op.rs:517-526, and ForAll/ForAny/IfExists variants share the same negative kinds/evaluators at condition_op.rs:257-305 and 333-355. In condition evaluation, variable-enabled operands are built with filter_map at crates/auth/src/bucket_policy/condition_key.rs:827-833, so an expansion returning None removes the operand. Unavailable/absent variable behavior is visible at bucket_policy.rs:1800-1808 and condition_key.rs:444-447 / 458-463 / 852-862. The negative evaluators then use operands.iter().all(...) with no empty-slice guard at condition_op.rs:1227-1232 for StringNotEquals and 1486-1491 for StringNotLike; all() is true for an empty iterator. The PoC uses public Allow ListBucket policies over s3:prefix. Output showed: StringEquals unavailable variable => NoMatch; StringNotEquals unavailable variable => ExplicitAllow; StringNotLike unavailable variable => ExplicitAllow; StringNotEquals absent variable with default, prefix=blocked => NoMatch; StringNotEquals absent variable without default, prefix=blocked => ExplicitAllow. lldb broke at auth::bucket_policy::condition_op::eval_string_not_equals and showed the call chain evaluate_clause -> eval_string_not_equals with operands.length = 0 while variables_enabled=true, confirming that filter_map dropped the only operand before the vacuous all() match.

